deploy is outside this repo.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"[challenge: unclear] CI publishes main artifacts with commit tags and production configuration pins a digest, but no production deployment workflow or deploy authorization configuration is supplied. CI-only deployment from a reviewed branch cannot be established."},{"id":"B12.4","check":"Branch protection config blocks force-pushes and history rewrites on the source branch.","verify":"1) Search for branch protection IaC (`github_branch_protection`, rulesets JSON). 2) Check force-push and deletion are blocked on the source branch. 3) Answer no if `allow_force_pushes: true` or bypass is open to the agent's identity. Answer unknown if protection is set only in repo settings and not in code.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"[challenge: unclear] The workflow selects main for pushes but does not protect its history. Hidden-file inventory and searches for branch protection, rulesets, force-push controls, and required reviews found no branch protection IaC. External repository settings are unavailable."}],"red_flags_found":["app/settings.py:61, app/startup.py:43, app/startup.py:46, app/main.py:107 The image includes prompts and config, but their runtime root is environment-controlled. Startup detects prompt and config hash mismatches without rejecting them, returns normally, and main loads the mismatching system prompt and policy. Thus loading only the reviewed build's versions is not enforced.",".github/workflows/ci.yaml:1 A complete hidden-file inventory of agent/ contains no CODEOWNERS; the sole .github file is this CI workflow, which has no required-review rules. Searches for CODEOWNERS, required reviews, branch protection, and rulesets found no review configuration. The quoted line identifies the inspected workflow; the failure is the absent control, not that line's content. The sheet explicitly says no CODEOWNERS is a failure."],"violates":["Version and review the harness, system prompt, and rules files before release.","Link the deployed versions to the approved review."],"also_affects":[],"summary":"Build prompts are fixed but release governance remains external.","next_step":"Supply protected branch review rules and production deployment audit records.","lead_role":"agent_team","challenge":{"verdict":"failed","note":"Reviewed artifact loading is not enforced and CODEOWNERS is absent; production deployment restrictions and source branch history protection cannot be determined from the supplied repository."}},{"item":"C01","gate":"G2","title":"Complete release manifest","requirement":"Write a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, also record the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and the digest of the output. The agent app's release number alone is not enough. Use the [model provenance fields](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#model-provenance-fields) to separate recorded versions from details a provider doesn't share. Check each model ID you request against the provider's versioning docs. Mark it as a pinned snapshot or as an alias that can change, and record which doc you used and its date. Naming rules differ by provider and model generation. Name each artifact's owner and a reference that can't change, where one exists. A machine-readable format, such as CycloneDX ML-BOM or the SPDX 3.0 AI and Dataset profiles, lets tools check the manifest.","not_covered":[{"problem":"Running setup not checked against the approved manifest","owner":"C03"},{"problem":"Manifest changes made without a reviewed diff and named author","owner":"C02"},{"problem":"Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed","owner":"C04"},{"problem":"Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs","owner":"A03"}],"rating":"gap","confidence":"medium","evidence":[{"file":"release/manifest.yaml","line":83,"note":"Manifest includes deployment artifacts tools policy scopes identities memory networking and budgets via explicit references."}],"checks":[{"id":"C01.1","check":"A release manifest file lists the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions.","verify":"1) Find the release manifest (`.brace/manifest.yaml`, release manifest, ML-BOM). 2) Check each category: container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval, identity bindings, network rules, budgets, security-policy versions. 3) Compare with what config sets up (`*.mcp.json`, NetworkPolicy). 4) Answer no if any category is missing or configured items are left out.","met":"yes","evidence":"release/manifest.yaml:83","quote":"budgets: config/policy.yaml#budgets","counter_search":"Compared all config, registry and deployment categories; rules live in system prompt, retrieval explicitly none.","note":"Manifest includes deployment artifacts tools policy scopes identities memory networking and budgets via explicit references."},{"id":"C01.2","check":"The manifest has a separate model record for every model (main, sub-agent, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers), each with provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version.","verify":"1) Search code for every model used: main, sub-agent, embedding, reranker, judge. 2) Check the manifest has a separate record for each. 3) Check each record has provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. 4) Answer no if any model in code has no record, or a record is missing a field.","met":"yes","evidence":"release/manifest.yaml:50","quote":"extractor:","counter_search":"Searched all model calls and manifests; no omitted embedding reranker or judge.","note":"Main and extractor have separate provider snapshot adapter fine-tune records."},{"id":"C01.3","check":"Each requested model ID is labeled as a pinned snapshot or a changeable alias, with the provider doc and date used.","verify":"1) Open each model record in the manifest. 2) Check it has a pinned-or-alias label, the provider doc used, and a date. 3) Answer no if any record lacks one of these, or labels an undated or `-latest` name as pinned.","met":"yes","evidence":"release/manifest.yaml:44","quote":"id_kind_source: \"Anthropic models overview, checked 2026-09-22\"","counter_search":"Checked both model records and deployment env; dated snapshot IDs, no alias labeled pinned. Provider docs not independently consulted due no-network constraint.","note":"Both requested snapshots have pinned labels and provider-doc/date claims."},{"id":"C01.4","check":"Each artifact has a named owner and a reference that can't change (digest or commit) where one exists, not a mutable tag or the app's release number.","verify":"1) For each manifest artifact, check for a named owner. 2) Check its reference is a digest (`sha256:`) or commit SHA where one exists. 3) Answer no if any artifact has no owner, or uses a mutable tag (`:latest`, branch name) or the app release number where a digest or commit was available.","met":"no","evidence":"release/manifest.yaml:34","quote":"network_policy: deploy/k8s/networkpolicy.yaml","counter_search":"","note":"Network and image policy artifacts referenced