on control, and merges require review by someone other than the named author.","verify":"1) Check the manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files are tracked in git, not loaded at runtime from an untracked path or remote URL. 2) Look for branch protection or ruleset config requiring approving review, and CODEOWNERS covering those paths. 3) Answer no if any of these files is untracked or not covered. Answer unknown if review rules are set only in the hosting service.","met":"no","evidence":"Dockerfile:17, Dockerfile:18, Dockerfile:19","quote":"COPY prompts /app/prompts\nCOPY config /app/config\nCOPY release/manifest.yaml /app/release/manifest.yaml","counter_search":"","note":"[challenge] These lines identify the packaged security-relevant paths. The complete hidden-file inventory under agent/ contains no CODEOWNERS, so none of these paths has the CODEOWNERS coverage explicitly required by this check. No branch-protection or ruleset configuration is supplied either; hosting-service review settings and git tracking cannot be verified from this snapshot. The failure is missing CODEOWNERS coverage, not a demonstrated bypass of external branch protection."},{"id":"C02.2","check":"Only named roles in config (CODEOWNERS, IAM, or protected CI environments) can change production configuration.","verify":"1) Open CODEOWNERS, IAM policies, and CI environment config (protected `environment:` entries). 2) Check production config paths and deploy jobs are limited to named roles. 3) Answer no if a catch-all `*` owner, a broad group, or the agent's own credentials can change production config, or a deploy job skips the protected environment.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"[challenge: unclear] An ownership label does not grant or restrict production changes. The supplied workflow builds and publishes releases but does not deploy production configuration. No IAM policy, Kubernetes role binding, protected deployment environment, or external production authorization configuration is supplied; neither named-role enforcement nor an unauthorized production-change path can be established."},{"id":"C02.3","check":"An emergency change path exists that records an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.","verify":"1) Search for an emergency or break-glass path (runbook, workflow, CLI). 2) Check it records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review. 3) Answer no if any of the four is missing, or the path bypasses review with no record. Answer unknown if the procedure lives outside this repo.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"[challenge: unclear] The operating documentation describes halt and release operations, but no emergency configuration-change procedure. Hidden-file inventory and searches for emergency, break-glass, follow-up, review, and end-date controls found no such path. Approval and token expiry fields concern business operations and credentials. An external emergency change-management procedure cannot be assessed, so no missing field in an actual emergency path is demonstrated."}],"red_flags_found":["Dockerfile:17, Dockerfile:18, Dockerfile:19 These lines identify the packaged security-relevant paths. The complete hidden-file inventory under agent/ contains no CODEOWNERS, so none of these paths has the CODEOWNERS coverage explicitly required by this check. No branch-protection or ruleset configuration is supplied either; hosting-service review settings and git tracking cannot be verified from this snapshot. The failure is missing CODEOWNERS coverage, not a demonstrated bypass of external branch protection."],"violates":["Require diffs with a named author, plus review, for changes to the manifest and its policies."],"also_affects":[],"summary":"Review and emergency production change controls are externally managed.","next_step":"Supply protected review rules, production deploy roles and break-glass records.","lead_role":"governance","challenge":{"verdict":"failed","note":"C02.1 fails the sheet's explicit CODEOWNERS coverage criterion; production authorization and emergency change procedures remain unclear from the supplied repository."}},{"item":"C03","gate":"G1","title":"Match release identity to running state","requirement":"Freeze the artifacts that define identity for each release. Check the running setup against the approved manifest, and emit the matching agent-type-id. Include the digest and signature of weights and adapters you host yourself. Test that a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup can't keep showing the old approved identity without being caught.","not_covered":[{"problem":"Manifest missing helper models, budgets, or other components","owner":"C01"},{"problem":"Hosted model changing behind a fixed name","owner":"C05"},{"problem":"Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs","owner":"A03"},{"problem":"Container image not signature-checked at admission","owner":"B10"}],"rating":"gap","confidence":"medium","evidence":[{"file":"deploy/k8s/deployment.yaml","line":32,"note":"Image digest pins built prompt/config files and env models are dated snapshots."}],"checks":[{"id":"C03.1","check":"Identity-defining artifacts are referenced in deployment config by digest or a version that can't change, not by mutable tags.","verify":"1) Open deployment config: Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, Terraform, compose files. 2) Check images use `@sha256:` and prompts, models, and weights use fixed versions. 3) Follow variables and defaults to the final value. 4) Answer no if any reference is a tag like `:latest`, a branch, a model alias, or a default that falls back to one.","met":"yes","evidence":"deploy/k8s/deployment.yaml:32","quote":"image: registry.example-corp.test/procurement/agent@sha256:7c1e4a0f9b3d2e85a6c4f1b0d9e8a7c6b5d4e3f2a1b0c9d8e7f6a5b4c3d2e1f0","counter_search":"Checked Dockerfile copies and all env model references; no remote mutable prompts or aliases.","note":"Image