ols a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.","not_covered":[{"problem":"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved","owner":"E03"},{"problem":"Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata","owner":"E04"},{"problem":"Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers","owner":"E05"},{"problem":"Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked","owner":"B10"}],"rating":"gap","confidence":"medium","evidence":[{"file":"pyproject.toml","line":6,"note":"Direct package versions fixed, but no full dependency lock or ERP server implementation pin supplied."}],"checks":[{"id":"E02.1","check":"Each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset is pinned by exact version or digest, with no floating tags, ranges, or 'latest'.","verify":"1) Open lockfiles, `*.mcp.json`, Dockerfiles, and model or dataset download code. 2) Check each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset uses an exact version or digest. 3) Answer no if any uses a range, `latest`, a branch, `npx` or `uvx` without a version, or an unpinned download URL.","met":"no","evidence":"pyproject.toml:6","quote":"dependencies = [","counter_search":"","note":"Direct package versions fixed, but no full dependency lock or ERP server implementation pin supplied."},{"id":"E02.2","check":"Signature checks run in the build or load path where the publisher provides signatures, and a failed check stops the install or load.","verify":"1) Find install and load steps in CI and code. 2) Check signature checks (`cosign verify`, `npm audit signatures`, sigstore, GPG) run where the publisher signs. 3) Answer no if a check is missing, its failure is ignored (`|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `try/except` that continues), or a second install path skips it.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"Read Dockerfile installs, CI signing and model config; image admission checks signatures, publisher package signature availability and external gateway artifact verification not supplied."},{"id":"E02.3","check":"A vulnerability scan of the pinned dependency list runs in CI and gates on the team's release policy.","verify":"1) Open CI workflows. 2) Find a vulnerability scan (`pip-audit`, `npm audit`, `osv-scanner`, `trivy`, `grype`) of the pinned dependency list. 3) Check it fails the build per the release policy. 4) Answer no if no scan exists, it is set not to fail (`--exit-zero`, `|| true`, `continue-on-error`), or it scans a different file than the lockfile actually used.","met":"no","evidence":".github/workflows/ci.yaml:21","quote":"- name: Secret scan","counter_search":"","note":"CI scans secrets and generates SBOM but has no dependency vulnerability release gate."},{"id":"E02.4","check":"Each approved dependency's record names its publisher, permissions, and update source, and remote services or unsigned artifacts have a recorded exception.","verify":"1) Find the dependency approval records. 2) Check each names publisher, permissions, and update source. 3) Check each remote service and unsigned artifact has a recorded exception. 4) Answer no if any approved dependency lacks these fields or the needed exception.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"Inspected release manifest README and config; dependency publisher/permission/update approval records and unsigned-service exceptions may live in external governance system, not supplied."}],"red_flags_found":[],"violates":["review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities"],"also_affects":[],"summary":"Dependency approval lacks full pinning and CI vulnerability gate.","next_step":"Lock complete artifacts and add release-policy vulnerability scan; supply approval records.","lead_role":"security"},{"item":"E03","gate":"G2","title":"Re-check tools on load","requirement":"Take a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.","not_covered":[{"problem":"Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved","owner":"E04"},{"problem":"Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions","owner":"E02"},{"problem":"A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one","owner":"E05"},{"problem":"Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts","owner":"E06"}],"rating":"gap","confidence":"medium","evidence":[{"file":"app/mcp_client.py","line":46,"note":"Fingerprint hashes names descriptions input schema annotations, compared to saved release pins."}],"checks":[{"id":"E03.1","check":"A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.","verify":"1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema.","met":"yes","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:46","quote":"\"inputSchema\": tool.get(\"inputSchema\", {}),","counter_search":"Checked fingerprint and manifest tool_pins; metadata hash not merely tool name.","note":"Fingerprint hashes names descriptions input schema annotations, compared to saved release pins."},{"id":"E03.2","check":"The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.","verify":"1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition.","met":"no","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:110","quote":"self._tools = {t[\"name\"]: t for t in tools}","counter_search":"","note":"Refresh on list_changed replaces cache without comparing pins or scanning metadata."},{"id":"E03.3","check":"When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.","verify":"1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cached definition. 3) Answer no if it only logs, uses the cached or new definition anyway, or an error during the check lets the tool load.","met":"yes","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:98","quote":"raise ToolPinMismatch","counter_search":"Checked initialize and its caller; no mismatch exception catch allows startup, refresh missing comparison recorded separately.","note":"Explicit startup pin mismatch raises before cache is stored, without cached fallback."},{"id":"E03.4","check":"The implementation version is pinned or attested where it can be seen, and recorded separately from the metadata fingerprint.","verify":"1) Check how each tool server implementation is referenced: package version, image digest, or attestation. 2) Check that version is recorded in its own field, apart from the metadata fingerprint. 3) Answer no if a visible implementation is unpinned, or only the metadata hash is recorded.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"Read ERP endpoint and release tool metadata hashes; remote implementation build version attestation and review schedule outside repository, cannot inspect gateway server code."}],"red_flags_found":[],"violates":["Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed"],"also_affects":["B01"],"summary":"MCP list refresh replaces approved cache without fingerprint checks.","next_step":"Reuse verified load routine on refresh and revoke cached definitions on failed checks.","lead_role":"infrastructure"},{"item":"E04","gate":"G2","title":"Inspect tool metadata","requirement":"Before the model sees them, scan server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates. Look for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and places data could be sent. Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious. Check that a scanner error doesn't quietly let unchecked metadata through.","not_covered":[{"problem":"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved","owner":"E03"},{"problem":"Hidden instructions in tool call results","owner":"R01"},{"problem":"Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers","owner":"E05"},{"problem":"Allow decisions based on server hints such as read-only","owner":"B01"}],"rating":"gap","confidence":"medium","evidence":[{"file":"app/mcp_client.py","line":99,"note":"Startup scans full serialized tool metadata before use; model schemas are local fixed release definitions."}],"checks":[{"id":"E04.1","check":"A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them.","verify":"1) Find where server instructions and tool metadata reach the model's context. 2) Check a scanner runs before that on every field: server instructions, names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, prompt and resource templates. 3) Answer no if any field, or a second load path, skips the scanner.","met":"yes","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:99","quote":"scan_metadata(tool)","counter_search":"Traced registry schemas: remote metadata is not copied into model prompts; refresh integrity failure separately filed E03.","note":"Startup scans full serialized tool metadata before use; model schemas are local fixed release definitions."},{"id":"E04.2","check":"The scanner checks for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and data-sending targets, and enforces length limits.","verify":"1) Open the scanner rules. 2) Check for each: hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters (zero-width, bidi), role overrides, encoded payloads (base64), data-sending targets (URLs, emails), and length limits. 3) Answer no if any one is missing.","met":"no","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:55","quote":"if len(description) > MAX_DESCRIPTION_CHARS:","counter_search":"","note":"Length enforced only on description, not names/schemas/templates; rules omit data-sending URLs/emails and many control characters."},{"id":"E04.3","check":"Metadata the scanner flags is cleaned or rejected, not just logged.","verify":"1) Open the scanner's result handling. 2) Check flagged metadata is cleaned, or the tool rejected, before the model sees it. 3) Answer no if flagged items are only logged or warned about and passed through.","met":"yes","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:60","quote":"raise ToolPinMismatch","counter_search":"Checked scan_metadata and initial caller; flagged output never merely logs and proceeds.","note":"Initial scanner-detected metadata rejected before tools are committed to cache."},{"id":"E04.4","check":"A scanner error or outage blocks the metadata instead of letting it through unchecked.","verify":"1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the scanner call. 2) Check an error blocks the metadata. 3) Answer no if any error branch passes metadata through, returns clean, or skips the scan, or a config flag disables scanning by default.","met":"yes","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:99","quote":"scan_metadata(tool)","counter_search":"Checked startup call stack; no catch that allows unchecked initial metadata, refresh recheck omission owned by E03.","note":"Scanner exceptions propagate and block startup rather than return clean."}],"red_flags_found":[],"violates":["Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious"],"also_affects":[],"summary":"Metadata scanner omits sending targets and comprehensive length limits.","next_step":"Bound and inspect every supported metadata field for all required attack categories.","lead_role":"security"},{"item":"E05","gate":"G2","title":"Verified, namespaced tools","requirement":"Identify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.","not_covered":[{"problem":"Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata","owner":"E04"},{"problem":"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved","owner":"E03"},{"problem":"Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message","owner":"E07"},{"problem":"Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action","owner":"E06"}],"rating":"gap","confidence":"medium","evidence":[{"file":"app/mcp_client.py","line":100,"note":"Remote lookup keyed by bare tool name; approved metadata pins omit verified endpoint identity."}],"checks":[{"id":"E05.1","check":"Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name.","verify":"1) Find the tool registry or lookup map. 2) Check its key is a verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace plus tool name. 3) Answer no if tools are keyed by bare tool name or the server's self-reported name (`serverInfo.name`), or a later server overwrites an earlier entry with the same tool name.","met":"no","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:100","quote":"self._tools = {t[\"name\"]: t for t in tools}","counter_search":"","note":"Remote lookup keyed by bare tool name; approved metadata pins omit verified endpoint identity."},{"id":"E05.2","check":"The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.","verify":"1) Open tool registration code. 2) Look for a check that compares new tool names across servers for duplicates and lookalikes (case, homoglyphs, separators). 3) Answer no if registration silently overwrites or accepts duplicates.","met":"no","evidence":"app/tools/__init__.py:66","quote":"available[tool.name] = tool","counter_search":"","note":"Registration silently overwrites duplicate tool names; remote dict comprehension likewise has no duplicate or lookalike detection."},{"id":"E05.3","check":"Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.","verify":"1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool.","met":"no","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:95","quote":"expected = self.pins.get(tool[\"name\"])","counter_search":"","note":"Approval pin bound to bare name/hash, not endpoint identity; alternate server with same metadata inherits pins."}],"red_flags_found":[],"violates":["Identify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name"],"also_affects":[],"summary":"Bare tool-name pins and silent overwrites weaken server-bound identity.","next_step":"Bind pins to verified endpoint and detect duplicate/lookalike names before registration.","lead_role":"infrastructure"},{"item":"E06","gate":"G2","title":"Enforced gateway boundary","requirement":"Send MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.","not_covered":[{"problem":"Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool","owner":"B01"},{"problem":"Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions","owner":"B02"},{"problem":"Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval","owner":"B05"},{"problem":"Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata","owner":"E04"},{"problem":"Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations","owner":"B09"}],"rating":"gap","confidence":"medium","evidence":[{"file":"app/mcp_client.py","line":70,"note":"One startup tools/list cache fetched using system identity is reused for every tenant and subject; no per-authorization-context cache key."}],"checks":[{"id":"E06.1","check":"The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.","verify":"1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"Read ERP gateway endpoint and NetworkPolicy: internal gateway namespace allowed without pod restriction, plus public HTTPS bypass; actual downstream server topology/credentials not supplied, no demonstrated direct server route."},{"id":"E06.2","check":"The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.","verify":"1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"Read gateway URL and client STS audience/scope requests; gateway token issuer/audience/expiry/scope validation and downstream credentials implemented outside repository."},{"id":"E06.3","check":"The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.","verify":"1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"Inspected gateway references and local RPC client; server-side authorization, validation, shared rate limits and decision logs are not supplied."},{"id":"E06.4","check":"If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.","verify":"1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"Read client raise_for_status and policy gate error flow; external gateway scanner/policy failure behavior is not supplied."},{"id":"E06.5","check":"Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.","verify":"1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.","met":"no","evidence":"app/mcp_client.py:70","quote":"self._tools: dict[str, dict[str, Any]] = {}","counter_search":"","note":"One startup tools/list cache fetched using system identity is reused for every tenant and subject; no per-authorization-context cache key."},{"id":"E06.6","check":"If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.","verify":"1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.","met":"unknown","evidence":"","quote":"","counter_search":"","note":"Searched gateway configuration, identity exchange and HTTP discovery; external gateway OAuth-client status, RFC9207 issuer check and discovery SSRF enforcement not supplied."}],"red_flags_found":[],"violates":["Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context"],"also_affects":[],"summary":"MCP tool cache is shared across tenant and subject authorization contexts.","next_step":"Partition cache by verified authorization context and obtain gateway enforcement evidence.","lead_role":"infrastructure"},{"item":"E07","gate":"G2","title":"Untrusted peer messages","requirement":"Authenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.","not_covered":[{"problem":"Tool server identified only by its self-reported name","owner":"E05"},{"problem":"Missing schema or size checks on API responses","owner":"R01"},{"problem":"Peers or children still running after a stop","owner":"E09"},{"problem":"Run can't